@startuml
title How a Waker wakes the task (not a specific child future)

participant "Executor" as EX
participant "Main task T" as T
participant "future1\n(lock waiter)" as F1
participant "future3\n(other branch)" as F3
participant "Mutex" as M
participant "Wait queue" as Q
participant "Stored Waker\n(for task T)" as W
participant "Background task" as BG

EX -> T: poll task
T -> F1: poll(cx)
note right of F1
cx contains a Waker for task T.
end note

F1 -> M: try lock
M -> Q: enqueue waiter for future1
Q -> W: store clone(cx.waker())
M --> F1: Pending
F1 --> T: Pending
T --> EX: task yields

== Later ==

BG -> M: unlock()
M -> Q: pop next waiter
Q -> W: wake()
W -> EX: mark task T runnable

EX -> T: poll task again

alt task polls future1 again
    T -> F1: poll(cx)
    F1 -> M: complete lock acquisition
    M --> F1: Ready(guard)
    F1 --> T: can make progress
else task has moved on and polls future3 only
    T -> F3: poll(cx)
    F3 --> T: Pending on same mutex
    note over T,F1
    The wake was correct: it woke task T.
    But T did not poll future1, so future1 stays stuck.
    end note
end

note over EX,T
The Future is the state machine.
The Waker is the notification handle that tells the executor
"please schedule this task to be polled again."
end note

@enduml
